After 5 draws you can have drawn at most 5 apples, and after 10 draws at most 10 apples. So the condition “apples drawn so far ≥ 13” is **impossible** at the only times it could affect future draws (after 5 and after 10). Hence, before the process ends at 15 draws, **no items are ever returned to the bag**.

Therefore the 15 observed draws are distributed exactly like drawing 15 items **without replacement** from the original bag of 57 fruits with 34 apples.

Let \(X\) be the number of apples drawn in 15 draws. Then \(X\) is hypergeometric with mean
\[
\mathbb E[X] \;=\; n\cdot \frac{K}{N}
\;=\; 15\cdot \frac{34}{57}
\;=\; \frac{510}{57}
\;=\; \frac{170}{19}
\approx 8.9474.
\]

So the expected number of apples drawn is \(\boxed{\frac{170}{19}\approx 8.9474}\).
